overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.509ex\" height=\"1.251ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -471 667 553\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2234\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2234\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> From 2 mole of electrons 1 mole of Ni is deposited at the cathode.\n<br><br>So from 0.1 F or 0.1 mole of electrons <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.781ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.452ex\" height=\"2.737ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864.9 3294 1209.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><rect width=\"553.6\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1015.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2016,0)\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\"></use><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> = 0.05 mole of Ni is deposited at the cathode."},"hi":{"content":"Ni(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> का एक घोल प्लेटिनम इलेक्ट्रोड्स के बीच 0.1 फैराडे विद्युत धारा का उपयोग करके विद्युत अपघटन किया जाता है। निकल के कितने मोल अवशेष कैथोड पर जमा होंगे?","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"0.10"},{"identifier":"B","content":"0.15"},{"identifier":"C","content":"0.20"},{"identifier":"D","content":"0.05"}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":null,"comprehension":null,"direction":null}},"question_id":"mSgr7enqEwoMXtcYZZlWu","permalink":"a-solution-of-nino32-is-electrolysed-between-platinum-elec-jee-main-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-qavqyekadh2tzsed","updated_time":1566508887,"timeAllotted":120000,"paperId":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","topic":"conductance-and-electrolysis","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":[],"yearKey":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","exam":"jee-main","subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"organic-chemistry","chapter":"hydrocarbons","year":2019,"paperTitle":"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"languages":["en"],"difficulty":"medium","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":"2019-04-09 14:30","question":{"en":{"content":"Increasing order of reactivity of the following\ncompounds for S<sub>N</sub>1 substitution is:\n<picture><source media=\"(max-width: 320px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrbj9ck/430b5223-9d71-4426-81a5-faea61fd24da/e27d1d40-c767-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrbj9cl-320w.jpg\"><source media=\"(max-width: 500px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrbj9ck/430b5223-9d71-4426-81a5-faea61fd24da/e27d1d40-c767-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrbj9cl-500w.jpg\"><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrbj9ck/430b5223-9d71-4426-81a5-faea61fd24da/e27d1d40-c767-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrbj9cl-680w.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot Chemistry - Hydrocarbons Question 133 English\"></picture>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"(A) &lt; (B) &lt; (D) &lt; (C)"},{"identifier":"B","content":"(B) &lt; (C) &lt; (D) &lt; (A)"},{"identifier":"C","content":"(B) &lt; (A) &lt; (D) &lt; (C)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"(B) &lt; (C) &lt; (A) &lt; (D)"}],"correct_options":["C"],"answer":null,"explanation":"In S<sub>N</sub>1 substitution reaction, reactivity of compound depends on the stability of carbocation formed in the rate determination step. Reactivity of compound will be more when it's carbocation is more stable.\n<br><br><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 320px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrdhjhy/1a11d835-bce3-4a69-a0d9-d9b4058741aa/84f81960-c76f-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrdhjhz-320w.jpg\"><source media=\"(max-width: 500px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrdhjhy/1a11d835-bce3-4a69-a0d9-d9b4058741aa/84f81960-c76f-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrdhjhz-500w.jpg\"><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrdhjhy/1a11d835-bce3-4a69-a0d9-d9b4058741aa/84f81960-c76f-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrdhjhz-680w.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot Chemistry - Hydrocarbons Question 133 English Explanation\"></picture>\n<br><br>So stability order of carbocation :\n<br><br>C &gt; D &gt; A &gt; B\n"},"hi":{"content":"निम्नलिखित यौगिकों की S<sub>N</sub>1 प्रतिस्थापन के लिए प्रतिक्रिया की बढ़ती क्रम है:\n<picture><source media=\"(max-width: 320px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrbj9ck/430b5223-9d71-4426-81a5-faea61fd24da/e27d1d40-c767-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrbj9cl-320w.jpg\"><source media=\"(max-width: 500px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrbj9ck/430b5223-9d71-4426-81a5-faea61fd24da/e27d1d40-c767-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrbj9cl-500w.jpg\"><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/jf76htyjzrbj9ck/430b5223-9d71-4426-81a5-faea61fd24da/e27d1d40-c767-11e9-8a74-7f4f47aa0c85/file-jf76htyjzrbj9cl-680w.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot Chemistry - Hydrocarbons Question 133 Hindi\"></picture>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"(A) &lt; (B) &lt; (D) &lt; (C)"},{"identifier":"B","content":"(B) &lt; (C) &lt; (D) &lt; (A)"},{"identifier":"C","content":"(B) &lt; (A) &lt; (D) &lt; (C)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"(B) &lt; (C) &lt; (A) &lt; (D)"}],"correct_options":["C"],"answer":null,"explanation":null,"comprehension":null,"direction":null}},"question_id":"itX4w5nABnhW9uESmXjTE","permalink":"increasing-order-of-reactivity-of-the-following-compounds-fo-jee-main-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-gzxeurw15jtl1b9y","updated_time":1566762814,"timeAllotted":120000,"paperId":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"topic":"reaction-of-alkenes","isBonus":false,"tags":["sequence_ordering"],"yearKey":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","exam":"jee-main","subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","chapter":"coordination-compounds","year":2019,"paperTitle":"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"languages":["en","hi"],"difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":"2019-04-09 14:30","question":{"en":{"content":"The correct statements among I to III are :<br>\n(I) Valence bond theory cannot explain the\ncolor exhibited by transition metal\ncomplexes.<br>\n(II) Valence bond theory can predict\nquantitatively the magnetic properties of\ntranstition metal complexes.<br>\n(III) Valence bond theory cannot distinguish\nligands as weak and strong field ones.","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"(II) and (III) only"},{"identifier":"B","content":"(I) and (II) only"},{"identifier":"C","content":"(I), (II) and (III)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"(I) and (III) only"}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>To determine which statements are correct, let's analyze each one individually in the context of <strong>Valence Bond Theory (VBT)</strong> as it applies to transition metal complexes.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3><strong>Statement (I):</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>\"Valence bond theory cannot explain the color exhibited by transition metal complexes.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Analysis:</strong></p>\n\n<p><p><strong>Color in Transition Metal Complexes:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>The colors of transition metal complexes arise from <strong>electronic transitions</strong> between different energy levels of the d-orbitals, specifically <strong>d-d transitions</strong>.</p></p>\n<p><p>These transitions occur when an electron absorbs light energy and moves from a lower-energy d-orbital to a higher-energy d-orbital.</p></p>\n\n<p><p><strong>Valence Bond Theory Limitations:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>VBT focuses on the <strong>hybridization of atomic orbitals</strong> to form covalent bonds.</p></p>\n<p><p>It does not account for the splitting of d-orbitals into different energy levels in the presence of ligands (known as <strong>crystal field splitting</strong>).</p></p>\n<p><p>Therefore, VBT <strong>cannot explain the origin of color</strong> in these complexes because it doesn't address the electronic transitions responsible for color.</p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I) is correct.</strong></p>\n\n<hr>\n<h3><strong>Statement (II):</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>\"Valence bond theory can predict quantitatively the magnetic properties of transition metal complexes.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Analysis:</strong></p>\n\n<p><p><strong>Magnetic Properties:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>The magnetic behavior of a complex depends on the number of <strong>unpaired electrons</strong> in the metal ion.</p></p>\n<p><p>Quantitative prediction requires calculating the <strong>magnetic moment</strong>, often using the formula:</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.639ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"42.21ex\" height=\"2.851ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -977.5 18657 1260\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(880.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msqrt\" transform=\"translate(1936.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0,67.5)\"><use data-c=\"221A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-SO-221A\"></use></g><g transform=\"translate(1020,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(600,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(989,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1811.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2811.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3311.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"3700.4\" height=\"60\" x=\"1020\" y=\"857.5\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\" transform=\"translate(6657,0)\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\"> </text></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\" transform=\"translate(7257,0)\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\">Bohr Magnetons (BM)</text></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>where <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.357ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 600 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is the number of unpaired electrons.</p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Valence Bond Theory Capabilities:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>VBT can provide a <strong>qualitative</strong> idea about the magnetic properties by indicating whether a complex is <strong>paramagnetic</strong> (unpaired electrons present) or <strong>diamagnetic</strong> (no unpaired electrons).</p></p>\n<p><p>However, VBT does not offer the tools to <strong>quantitatively</strong> predict the exact magnetic moment.</p></p>\n<p><p>Accurate quantitative predictions require more advanced theories like <strong>Crystal Field Theory (CFT)</strong> or <strong>Ligand Field Theory (LFT)</strong>.</p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (II) is incorrect.</strong></p>\n\n<hr>\n<h3><strong>Statement (III):</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>\"Valence bond theory cannot distinguish ligands as weak and strong field ones.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Analysis:</strong></p>\n\n<p><p><strong>Weak and Strong Field Ligands:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>Ligands are classified based on their ability to split the d-orbitals of the metal ion, influencing the pairing of electrons.</p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Strong field ligands</strong> cause a large splitting, often leading to low-spin complexes.</p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Weak field ligands</strong> cause small splitting, leading to high-spin complexes.</p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Valence Bond Theory Limitations:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>VBT does not address the energy splitting of d-orbitals.</p></p>\n<p><p>It assumes that all bonds are formed via overlap of orbitals without considering the effect of ligands on d-orbital energies.</p></p>\n<p><p>Therefore, VBT <strong>cannot distinguish between weak and strong field ligands</strong> because it doesn't involve the spectrochemical series or orbital splitting concepts.</p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (III) is correct.</strong></p>\n\n<hr>\n<h3><strong>Final Answer:</strong></h3>\n\n<p><p><strong>Statements (I) and (III) are correct.</strong></p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Statement (II) is incorrect.</strong></p></p>\n\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Option D</p>\n<p><strong>(I) and (III) only</strong></p>"},"hi":{"content":"सही कथन I से III में से हैं :<br>\n(I) वैलेंस बॉन्ड सिद्धांत संक्रमण धातु संयुक्तों द्वारा प्रदर्शित रंग को समझा नहीं सकता।<br>\n(II) वैलेंस बॉन्ड सिद्धांत संक्रमण धातु संयुक्तों के चुंबकीय गुणों की मात्रा में भविष्यवाणी कर सकता है।<br>\n(III) वैलेंस बॉन्ड सिद्धांत कमजोर और मजबूत क्षेत्र वाले लिगेंडों में अंतर नहीं कर सकता।","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"(II) और (III) केवल"},{"identifier":"B","content":"(I) और (II) केवल"},{"identifier":"C","content":"(I), (II) और (III)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"(I) और (III) केवल"}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":null,"comprehension":null,"direction":null}},"question_id":"EqgJ5MWVQbKw7AJZzYit0","permalink":"the-correct-statements-among-i-to-iii-are-i-valence-bond-jee-main-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-mhv8noghzxkm8kdc","updated_time":1566842850,"timeAllotted":120000,"paperId":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","topic":"warners-theory-and-valence-bond-theory","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":["multi_statements"],"yearKey":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","active":true}],"ids":["YNKUz0vHr32WvL7ebJTXB","mSgr7enqEwoMXtcYZZlWu","itX4w5nABnhW9uESmXjTE","EqgJ5MWVQbKw7AJZzYit0"],"title":"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot | JEE Main Year Wise Previous Years Questions","canonical":"https://app/past-years/jee/the-correct-statements-among-i-to-iii-regarding-group-13-ele-jee-main-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-99l7tme4gzxfcrf4"}